1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,305
Laddoo Lab has ads, offers and ten shops.

2
00:00:03,305 --> 00:00:06,012
But few people know the story behind it.

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:09,134
Then a local reporter
visits Nani's kitchen.

4
00:00:09,684 --> 00:00:12,324
The next morning, Nani
is in the newspaper.

5
00:00:12,324 --> 00:00:14,096
And that week, orders jump.

6
00:00:14,746 --> 00:00:16,885
Zoya did not pay for that story.

7
00:00:16,885 --> 00:00:17,949
She earned it.

8
00:00:18,299 --> 00:00:23,000
The reporter came because the story was
real: a grandmother's millet recipe,

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,076
made by hand.

10
00:00:24,426 --> 00:00:27,736
This is the power of
public relations, or PR.

11
00:00:28,386 --> 00:00:30,311
So what is public relations?

12
00:00:30,611 --> 00:00:34,760
Kotler and Armstrong call it building
good relations with the company's

13
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:39,254
publics, by earning good publicity,
building a good image, and handling

14
00:00:39,254 --> 00:00:40,920
bad rumours and stories.

15
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:45,482
A public is any group that cares about
your brand: customers, neighbours,

16
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:48,168
the press, shopkeepers,
even the government.

17
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:51,505
Let's see how the Nani
story really happened.

18
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:52,755
Step one.

19
00:00:52,753 --> 00:00:54,275
Find a true story.

20
00:00:54,275 --> 00:00:58,207
Dev notices that people love
hearing about Nani's recipe.

21
00:00:58,557 --> 00:00:59,457
Step two.

22
00:00:59,415 --> 00:01:00,598
Write it down.

23
00:01:00,598 --> 00:01:05,277
Dev writes a short press release,
a one-page news note for reporters.

24
00:01:05,627 --> 00:01:06,646
Step three.

25
00:01:06,646 --> 00:01:08,063
Invite the press.

26
00:01:08,063 --> 00:01:12,225
Zoya asks a local reporter to
a tasting in Nani's kitchen.

27
00:01:12,575 --> 00:01:13,483
Step four.

28
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:14,713
The story runs.

29
00:01:14,713 --> 00:01:18,895
A photo of Nani, and a headline
about healthy tiffin sweets.

30
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:20,145
Step five.

31
00:01:20,009 --> 00:01:20,909
Share it.

32
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:24,532
Dev posts the story, and every
shop sticks it up by the counter.

33
00:01:25,182 --> 00:01:26,418
Ads are paid for.

34
00:01:26,418 --> 00:01:27,978
Good stories are earned.

35
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:31,692
A story in the news is just one PR tool.

36
00:01:31,692 --> 00:01:33,210
Here are three more.

37
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:34,460
Events.

38
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:37,692
Laddoo Lab runs a millet
tasting at a school fair.

39
00:01:38,042 --> 00:01:39,201
Community work.

40
00:01:39,201 --> 00:01:42,598
Nani teaches a free
healthy-snack class for parents.

41
00:01:42,948 --> 00:01:44,239
And social media.

42
00:01:44,239 --> 00:01:48,162
Dev answers every question from
parents, kindly and quickly.

43
00:01:48,812 --> 00:01:50,779
Where do we see this at work today?

44
00:01:51,129 --> 00:01:52,029
News.

45
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:55,636
A hospital shares its new heart
unit with the local paper.

46
00:01:55,986 --> 00:01:56,886
Events.

47
00:01:56,642 --> 00:02:00,066
A bank runs a free money
class for college students.

48
00:02:00,416 --> 00:02:01,539
Community work.

49
00:02:01,539 --> 00:02:05,260
A bakery gives its extra bread
to a shelter every night.

50
00:02:05,610 --> 00:02:07,041
Handling bad news.

51
00:02:07,041 --> 00:02:10,462
An airline explains a
delay quickly and honestly.

52
00:02:11,112 --> 00:02:13,766
But is PR always in your control?

53
00:02:13,766 --> 00:02:14,666
No.

54
00:02:14,619 --> 00:02:15,648
The good side.

55
00:02:15,648 --> 00:02:19,545
People trust a news story more
than an ad, and it costs little.

56
00:02:19,895 --> 00:02:21,053
The hard side.

57
00:02:21,053 --> 00:02:23,624
You can't control what reporters write.

58
00:02:23,624 --> 00:02:25,520
And bad news spreads fast.

59
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:28,912
One day, a parent posts
that a pack was stale.

60
00:02:28,912 --> 00:02:32,853
Zoya replies that same day, says
sorry, and replaces the pack.

61
00:02:33,203 --> 00:02:35,936
Telling the truth fast is an old idea.

62
00:02:35,936 --> 00:02:40,977
In 1906, after a train crash in America,
Ivy Lee told the railway to share

63
00:02:40,977 --> 00:02:42,786
the facts with reporters.

64
00:02:42,786 --> 00:02:46,413
His statement is often called
the first press release.

65
00:02:46,763 --> 00:02:47,663
The lesson?

66
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,300
Earn trust with the truth, most
of all when things go wrong.

67
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:52,898
Quick check.

68
00:02:52,898 --> 00:02:57,157
A reporter writes about Nani's
kitchen, and Laddoo Lab pays nothing.

69
00:02:57,157 --> 00:02:58,564
Which tool is this?

70
00:03:00,464 --> 00:03:01,593
Public relations!

71
00:03:01,593 --> 00:03:03,777
The story was earned, not bought.

72
00:03:04,377 --> 00:03:05,387
So, remember.

73
00:03:05,687 --> 00:03:09,927
Public relations builds good relations
with your publics, and earns good

74
00:03:09,927 --> 00:03:10,827
stories.

75
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:15,322
Its tools include news, events,
community work and social media.

76
00:03:15,672 --> 00:03:18,472
And when things go wrong,
tell the truth, fast.

77
00:03:19,072 --> 00:03:21,828
Next time: marketing planning and control.
